From 85dc5a5a85788170c00d23294fba7a703b63840f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 22:44:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09314/11362] Probe both ends of a window for goal defects, and
 keep the scored server log

TWO CHANGES, both from tonight's A/B findings.

THE GOAL PROBE READ THE WRONG END. `grids[:_GOAL_PROBE_MAX_GRIDS]` takes the first 12
grids, which at (grid, next_grid) per transition is the first SIX transitions. Every
window is built to END at its level flip, so the win frame is always the LAST grid, and
the win was visible only for windows of six transitions or fewer. On longer ones a CORRECT
predicate reads constant-False across everything the probe sees and is rejected, while one
that fires early on a non-win frame reads non-constant and is kept -- the gate rejected
correct predicates and rewarded false positives, on exactly the windows where induction is
hardest. 7 of 13 games in window_meta.json exceed six transitions.

The fix is not a bigger cap, which would repair the symptom until some window outgrew it
while still pointing at the wrong end. `_goal_probe_sample` splits the same budget between
head and tail, so the win frame is included at any length, early-frame coverage is kept,
and cost is unchanged -- the cap is what bounds the hang exposure the caller documents.
Windows at or under the cap probe exactly what they always did.

THE SCORED SERVER LOG DIED WITH THE RUN. `_ensure_server` already writes llama.cpp's
stderr under CARNOT_ARC_SERVER_LOG_DIR, but the default is the container's /tmp. The
kernel now points it at /kaggle/working and the vLLM launch honours the same knob. Three
failed scored runs were undiagnosable for exactly this reason until one log was copied out
by hand. It also answers the open question behind tonight's timeout analysis -- real
induce durations are recoverable from the server's own `eval time` lines, with no
instrumentation of the Python at all.

VERIFICATION, INCLUDING ITS LIMIT. 16 new tests cover the sampling directly, including the
24-grid case that is the incident's exact shape. Against HEAD over the same selection:
15 failed / 35 passed before, 15 failed / 51 passed after -- the same 15, plus these 16.
Those 15 are a PRE-EXISTING red suite over the defect-gate reask wiring, filed separately.
So regression coverage around this change is weaker than it looks: the wiring tests that
would catch a break in the surrounding path are already failing and could not.

